  browser sketch so you can compare where the filters could go.

  Two things I'll come back to before the design, because they affect scope:
  - [1mNo shared token sheet:[0m the guidelines say page CSS must use tokens from a shared token sheet, but [38;5;153mpublic/[39m doesn't
    have one.
  - [1mBlank without JavaScript:[0m the table is built entirely by [38;5;153mactivity.js[39m, so the page shows no events when JavaScript is
    off. That already breaks the rule in your notes.

  First, which filters to build. The only support tickets so far were people hunting for a failed sign-in they didn't
  recognize, and Security wants those easy to find. Whatever we pick, the event-type filter has nine fixed options, and
  I'd add a one-click "Sign-in failed" shortcut. The options differ in how much markup and code they add, since the diff
  has to stay under about 300 lines:

  - [1mEvent type only:[0m the smallest diff and covers the ticket case. It doesn't help someone who knows roughly when
    something happened.
  - [1mEvent type plus date range:[0m two date pickers, a presets select and the invalid-range message the guidelines require.
    That's the most new code of the three, and the time-zone handling has to be right.
  - [1mEvent type plus text search over device and location:[0m one search box with a clear button, and it finds "Firefox" or
    "Unknown" fast. Less code than a date range.

  I'd go with event type plus search. The shortcut covers what Security wants, and search covers "that wasn't my
  device". All 500 events fit on one screen when filtered, so a date range adds the most code for the least gain. Adding
  a date range later is cheap.
